## Core Scoring Rubric

Each candidate receives a 0–100 score.

Hiring Signal Score: 20 points

Measures whether the project maps to real job requirements.

Portfolio Artifact Score: 15 points

Measures whether the project produces a recruiter-readable artifact.

Sandbox Feasibility Score: 15 points

Measures whether the project can run safely in Atlas without expensive external resources.

Validation Strength Score: 15 points

Measures whether Atlas can verify meaningful output.

Role Coverage Score: 10 points

Measures whether the project fills a role-path gap.

Cloud / Platform Relevance Score: 10 points

Measures relevance to AWS, Azure, GCP, Snowflake, Databricks, dbt, Spark, Airflow, Kafka, MLflow, Bedrock, Vertex AI, Azure OpenAI, and similar tools.

Differentiation Score: 10 points

Measures whether the project is better than generic online tutorials.

Build Risk Score: 5 points

Measures implementation risk. Lower risk gets higher score.

## Acceptance Bands

> 90–100: flagship candidate
> 80–89: strong candidate
> 70–79: acceptable if strategic
> 60–69: revise
> below 60: reject

## End-to-End Workflow

Step 1: Market signal collection

Market Signal Agent collects job postings, company career pages, cloud provider docs, GitHub trends, industry blogs, and competitor course catalogs.

Step 2: Atlas catalog gap analysis

Catalog Analyst Agent checks discipline coverage, difficulty distribution, validation gaps, cloud platform coverage, SQL/Python coverage, and duplicate topics.

Step 3: Candidate generation

Project Scout Agent proposes candidate projects with title, slug, discipline, scenario, tools, final artifact, validation idea, sandbox feasibility, and BYO-cloud extension.

Step 4: Deduplication

Deduplication Agent checks overlap in title, slug, skills, final artifact, role path, and difficulty tier.

Step 5: Feasibility analysis

Feasibility Agent determines whether the project can run in Pyodide, DuckDB-WASM, local dbt, mock cloud, or BYO-cloud.

Step 6: Validation strategy

Validation Architect Agent designs validation type per step and determines whether the project requires signed runtime output, server-side comparison, structured contains, exact match, SQL result-set comparison, CSV comparison, or self-attestation.

Step 7: Hiring-manager scoring

Hiring Manager Reviewer Agent evaluates whether a recruiter would care about the project.

Step 8: Portfolio scoring

Portfolio Artifact Agent determines whether the output can become a GitHub repo, README, diagram, LinkedIn post, resume bullet, and interview artifact.

Step 9: Ranking

Scoring Agent combines all scores and recommends projects to author.

Step 10: Human approval

Human operator approves the project wave.

Step 11: Authoring

Project Author Agent writes hidden authored project modules.

Step 12: Audit

Automated scripts run authoring, pedagogy, difficulty, validation, and copy-safety checks.

Step 13: Red-team review

Red-Team Reviewer Agent checks for weak validation, fake cloud claims, answer leakage, cost risk, and overclaims.

Step 14: Manual checklist

Human or trusted reviewer confirms publish-readiness.

Step 15: Visibility flip

Publisher Agent flips `learner_visible=true` only after approval.

