# SYSTEM 6: VALIDATION MATURITY SYSTEM

## System Name

Validation Maturity System

## Product Purpose

This system classifies each validation path by trust level so Atlas knows what it can honestly claim.

## Why This System Exists

Atlas cannot treat self-attestation, substring checks, signed runtime outputs, and deterministic server execution as equivalent. Different validation types support different claim levels.

## Tech Stack

Recommended implementation:

* TypeScript validation classifier
* validation-kind matrix document
* project validation score computation
* admin validation dashboard
* certificate claim guard
* copy-safety integration

## Maturity Levels

> Level 0: self-attest
> Level 1: contract-shaped metadata
> Level 2: thin server check
> Level 3: structured server check
> Level 4: client runtime feedback
> Level 5: signed runtime output
> Level 6: server-executed deterministic validation
> Level 7: reviewed portfolio artifact + runtime evidence

## Example Project Validation Summary

> This project has:
>
> * 3 structured server checks
> * 2 signed runtime checks
> * 1 portfolio artifact check

## Core Workflow

Step 1: Inspect every project step.

Step 2: Classify validation kind.

Step 3: Compute project validation maturity score.

Step 4: Store or derive validation summary.

Step 5: Use summary in admin dashboard and certificate claim guard.

Step 6: Prevent overclaiming.

## API Endpoints

Recommended:

> GET /api/admin/validation/maturity
> Returns validation maturity distribution.

> GET /api/projects/:slug/validation-summary
> Returns internal project validation summary.

## Acceptance Criteria

This system is complete when:

* every validation kind has maturity classification
* every project has computed maturity
* certificate copy is constrained by maturity
* admin can see weak validation hotspots
* project authoring can reject weak validation patterns

